        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Bien has been with you since chapter one, and so has bueno."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both have an opposite, and the pair works exactly the same way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart: mal",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malo is the adjective — bad. Mal is what is left of it when it goes in front of a masculine noun, and it is also the adverb — badly."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "bien / mal, well / badly.",
                "bueno / malo, good / bad."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Estoy mal. — I'm not well."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Both come straight from Latin: bene and malus, which you can still hear in English malady and malice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now look at what has happened twice in three chapters."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "long form",
                "short form",
                "short one appears"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "long form: mucho. short form: muy. short one appears: before an adjective or adverb.",
                "long form: malo. short form: mal. short one appears: before a masculine noun, and as the adverb."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is not two exceptions. It is one habit of the language: Spanish bites the ending off a word that is leaning on another word, and keeps it whole when it stands on its own."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Once you have heard it twice you will start noticing it everywhere, and it stops being something to memorise. A short form is a word in a hurry to get to the next one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "bien, then mal",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *bien*, then *mal*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "mucho / muy, then malo / mal, and hear the same trick twice",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *mucho* / *muy*, then *malo* / *mal*, and hear the same trick twice]"
            },
            {
